course: religion-ethics
lesson: 76
title: Living well because life is short
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: After Grandad's funeral Ellie's mum says, «Life is short. Let's go to the seaside this weekend, like Grandad always wanted»: a death often reminds people that life does not last for ever, and makes them want to spend it on what matters.
explain: Lucas's grandad left a list called «Things I'm glad I did»: «planted trees», «said sorry to my brother», «learned to dance»; many people near the end of life say they wish they had loved more and worried less, not that they had owned more.
explain: Ruby asks, «If we're all going to die anyway, what's the point of anything?», but a birthday party does not last for ever and is still worth having: most people find the point in love, friendship, learning, helping and enjoying the world.
explain: Christians believe each day is a gift from God and that love is the greatest thing, as Paul wrote, «Faith, hope and love, and the greatest of these is love»; humanists say this life is the one we know, so we should make it count; both say live well now.
explain: Old sayings carry the same wisdom: «Don't let the sun go down on your anger» (from the Bible) means make up before bed; «a good innings» (from cricket) means a long, full life; a gravestone that says only «She loved and was loved» says what lasts.
explain: For a child today this means: don't wait to tell people you love them, to say sorry, or to do good things together.
fact: A death often reminds people that life is short and makes them want to spend time on what matters.
fact: Many people near the end of life say they wish they had loved more and worried less.
fact: Things do not have to last for ever to matter; love, friendship, learning and helping give life meaning for most people.
fact: Christians believe life has meaning because God made and loves each person, and that life continues with God.
fact: Humanists believe meaning comes from how we live and love in this one life.
fact: The poem «Do not stand at my grave and weep», usually credited to Mary Elizabeth Frye in 1932, says the dead live on in nature and memory; it comforts many people.
fact: «A good innings» is a saying from cricket that means a long, full life.
fact: «Don't let the sun go down on your anger» comes from the Bible (Ephesians 4:26) and means make up before the day ends.
fact: A bucket list is a list of things someone wants to do before they die; the name comes from the saying «kick the bucket», meaning «die».
fact: Psalm 90 says, «Teach us to number our days, that we may apply our hearts unto wisdom»: it asks God to help people remember that life is short, so they live wisely.
fact: Paul wrote, «Faith, hope and love, and the greatest of these is love.»
fact: Most people who love us would want us to keep living fully and be happy after they die.
fact: Saying «I love you» often is a good habit, not a weird one.
exercise: After Grandad's funeral, why does Ellie's mum want a trip to the seaside? | A death reminds her life is short, so she wants to do what matters | She wants to forget Grandad as fast as possible | The funeral rules say families must go away | She is glad that the sad time is finally over
exercise: What was on Lucas's grandad's list «Things I'm glad I did»? | Planting trees and saying sorry to his brother | Buying the biggest, shiniest car in the whole street | Winning every argument he ever had | Never telling anyone his feelings
exercise: What do many people near the end of life say they wish? | That they had loved more and worried less | That they had worked many more hours | That they had bought many more things | That they had argued much more often
exercise: Ruby asks, «If we all die anyway, what's the point?» What do most people answer? | Love, friendship, learning and helping matter now | There really is no point to anything at all, so why bother trying | Only things that last for ever matter | The point is to collect as much as possible
exercise: Why can a short thing still matter? | Like a birthday party, it's good even though it ends | Short things never matter to anyone | Only things that last a million years or more really count for anything | Things matter only if they are expensive
exercise: What does the poem «Do not stand at my grave and weep» say? | The person lives on in nature and memories | The person is angry that nobody ever comes to visit | People must never cry at a grave | The person is trapped inside the grave
exercise: Sophie's gran says, «I've had a good innings. No regrets.» What does «a good innings» mean? | A long, full life | A good cricket match | A cosy house | A large meal
exercise: Where does the saying «a good innings» come from? | Cricket | Football | The Bible | Chess
exercise: Lily's dad says Mum would want them to keep living fully. What is true? | Most people who love us want us to live happily | The dead want their families to stay sad for ever, to show their love | Living happily means forgetting the dead | Nobody can know anything about love
exercise: Ethan's grandad says, «Don't let the sun go down on your anger.» What does it mean? | Make up before the day ends | Stay indoors when the sun sets | Be angry only in the daytime | Never go out in strong sunshine
exercise: Where does «Don't let the sun go down on your anger» come from? | The Bible | A cricket rule book | A weather report | A pop song
exercise: Oscar asks why people make bucket lists. What is a bucket list? | Things you want to do before you die | Jobs to do with a mop and bucket | A list of things to take to the beach | A shopping list for a building site
exercise: What does «kick the bucket» mean? | To die | To get angry | To win a race | To clean up
exercise: Zoe sees a gravestone saying «She loved and was loved». What does it suggest matters most? | Love | Money | Fame | Prizes
exercise: Who wrote «Faith, hope and love, and the greatest of these is love»? | Paul, in the Bible | Jesus, at the Last Supper | Moses, on the mountain | A modern poet
exercise: Mia says she'll tell her mum she loves her every day now. Is that weird? | No; it's a lovely habit | Yes; it would annoy her mum | Yes; once a year is enough | No, but only on birthdays
test: «If we're all gonna die anyway, what's even the point, it's giving nihilism,» says Ruby's teen brother in LA (it's giving = it feels like; nihilism = «nothing matters»). Which is the wisest answer? | Things don't have to last for ever to matter; love and kindness count now | He's right, nothing is worth doing at all | The point is to get rich before you die | Only grown-ups' lives have a point
test: «Me nan says she's had a good innings, is she on about cricket?» asks a lad in Yorkshire. What does she mean? | She's had a long, full life she's happy with | She used to play cricket for England when she was a young woman | She wants to watch the cricket now | She's about to go into hospital
test: The vicar reads, very formally: «Teach us to number our days, that we may apply our hearts unto wisdom.» What does this Psalm ask? | Help us remember life is short, so we live wisely | Teach us to count well in maths lessons | Tell us the exact day we will die, so that we can get ready for it | Help us live for ever on earth
test: What would happen if Ethan and his brother made up before bed every time they argued? | They'd waste fewer days being angry | They'd never be friends again | Their anger would grow every night | They'd have to stop playing together
test: «A bucket list? Like, a list of buckets? That's mid,» says a Gen Alpha cousin (mid = boring). What is it really? | Things you want to do before you die | A list of all the buckets you want to buy | A list of chores for cleaning the house | A game that people play on the beach in summer
test: «Grandad never said 'love you' much but he took us fishing every arvo,» says Brooke in Brisbane (arvo = afternoon). Did he show love? | Yes; love shows in what people do, not just words | No; love only counts if it is said out loud, in words | No; fishing has nothing to do with love | Only if he also bought them presents
test: A boy in Boston says, «Living like there's no tomorrow means skipping school and eating candy all day, wicked!» (wicked = great). What's a wiser idea? | Live well: love people, learn, help, enjoy good things | Yes, never do anything boring again | Nothing matters, so do whatever you like | Wait until you are old to enjoy life
test: In a cartoon, a rich king's gravestone lists all his gold. A poor woman's says «She loved and was loved». Which life sounds better lived? | The woman's; love is what lasts | The king's; gold is what lasts | Neither; gravestones mean nothing | The king's; more words means more life
test: «Mum wants us to be happy?? She's dead, how'd she even know,» snaps Lily. What's the gentle answer? | Most people who love us would want us to live happily | She'd want you to stay sad for ever and never smile again, to show you miss her | Being happy means you've forgotten her | Nobody can say anything about her wishes
test: «Life's short, so I'm gonna tell my mum I love her every day now, is that cringe?» asks Mia. Best answer? | Not cringe at all; it's a lovely habit | Yes, saying it more makes it mean less | Yes, only babies say that to mums | Only say it on her birthday
test: Myth to check: «Thinking about death makes life gloomy and pointless.» | False; it can help people live more fully and kindly | True; you should never think about it, not even once | True, because nothing can last for ever | False, but only for very old people
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the regrets commonly expressed by persons near the end of life? | That they had not loved more nor worried less | That they had not acquired greater wealth | That they had not engaged in more disputes | That they had not worked longer hours
exam: Identify the signification of the expression «a good innings» as applied to a human life. | A long and full life | A successful sporting career | A substantial inheritance | A comfortable dwelling
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the precept «Do not let the sun go down on your anger». | It derives from the Epistle to the Ephesians and counsels reconciliation before the day's end | It derives from the rules of cricket and concerns the close of play | It counsels that anger be expressed only after sunset | It prohibits outdoor activity during the hours of daylight
exam: Whereas it is asserted that the inevitability of death renders all endeavour pointless, ascertain the response most consistent with the matters taught. | Things of limited duration, such as love and kindness, retain their worth | All endeavour is indeed pointless and ought to be abandoned | Only endeavours of permanent duration possess any worth | Worth is to be measured solely by accumulated possessions
